---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import bvh from '../../data/path-tracer-bvh.json';
import sampling from '../../data/path-tracer-sampling.json';
import threads from '../../data/path-tracer-threads.json';
const rows = threads.summary.filter(r => r.rng === 'pixel' && r.scheduler === 'row');
const baseline = rows.find(r => r.threads === 1)!;
const best = rows.find(r => r.threads === 4)!;
const samples = sampling.summary.filter(r => r.kind === 'spp' && r.target_spp === 64);
const labels = { uniform: 'Uniform hemisphere', ndf: 'GGX NDF', vndf: 'GGX VNDF' };
const chapters = [['01', 'Geometry', 'geometry'], ['02', 'Acceleration', 'acceleration'], ['03', 'Reflection', 'reflection'], ['04', 'Performance', 'parallelism'], ['05', 'Architecture', 'architecture']];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><BaseHead title="CPU Path Tracer — Chao Bian" description="Extending RTIOW into a C++ CPU renderer: SAH BVH, GGX microfacet reflection, triangle meshes, and measured parallel performance." /></head>
<body>
  <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
  <header class="site-nav"><a class="wordmark" href="/">CHAO BIAN<span> / SELECTED WORK</span></a><a href="/#projects">All projects ↗</a></header>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="hero wrap">
      <div class="kicker"><span>RENDERING / ENGINEERING STUDY</span><span>C++ · CPU</span></div>
      <h1>CPU Path Tracer<span>Beyond the first image.</span></h1>
      <div class="hero-copy"><p>A CPU renderer extended from <em>Ray Tracing in One Weekend</em> with triangle meshes, SAH acceleration, GGX microfacet reflection, and multithreaded rendering.</p></div>
      <figure class="hero-image"><img src="/projects/path-tracer/microfacet.png" alt="Red dielectric and gold metallic spheres showing progressively rougher GGX reflections" width="480" height="320" fetchpriority="high" /><figcaption><span>FIG. 01 / MATERIAL RESPONSE</span><span>GGX reflection · dielectric & metallic surfaces</span></figcaption></figure>
      <div class="hero-bottom"><p>Graphics theory.<br />Systems performance.<br />Engineering implementation.</p><p>Starting from the Ray Tracing in One Weekend series, I extended a CPU path tracer to support imported meshes, SAH BVH acceleration, GGX microfacet reflection, and parallel rendering. Along the way, I compared alternative implementations to better understand their performance and tradeoffs.</p><a href="#overview">Read the case study ↓</a></div>
    </header>
    <nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Case study chapters"><div class="wrap">{chapters.map(([n,label,id]) => <a href={`#${id}`}><span>{n}</span>{label}</a>)}</div></nav>
    <section id="overview" class="wrap foundation">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">THE STARTING POINT</p><h2>A foundation to<br />build beyond.</h2></div>
      <div><p>The project began with Peter Shirley’s <a href="https://raytracing.github.io/">Ray Tracing in One Weekend series</a>: recursive Monte Carlo integration, material scattering, emissive lighting, PDFs and importance sampling, and basic AABB/BVH traversal.</p><p>That working renderer became the testbed for my own extensions. The focus here is on the decisions that followed: handling imported geometry, improving spatial partitions, sampling rough reflections, and understanding CPU scaling.</p><div class="feature-line">C++ / CPU PATH TRACING / MULTITHREADING / TRIANGLE MESHES / SAH BVH / GGX BRDF</div></div>
    </section>
    <section id="geometry" class="geometry-section"><div class="wrap">
      <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / TRIANGLE MESH & ASSET PIPELINE</p><h2>Geometry is more than<br />a list of triangles.</h2><p>To render models beyond procedural primitives, I added triangle intersection and OBJ/MTL loading. Barycentric coordinates let me interpolate texture coordinates and vertex normals at each hit, while per-triangle bounds connect imported meshes to the existing BVH system.</p></header>
      <div class="mesh-layout"><figure><img src="/projects/path-tracer/spot.png" alt="Spot cow mesh rendered with barycentrically interpolated texture coordinates" width="480" height="480" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FIG. 02 / SPOT · 5,856 TRIANGLES · DIFFUSE MTL TEXTURE</figcaption></figure><div class="triangle-study">
        <p class="eyebrow">AT THE HIT POINT / SCHEMATIC</p>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 440 310" role="img" aria-labelledby="triangle-title triangle-desc"><title id="triangle-title">Barycentric surface interpolation</title><desc id="triangle-desc">A ray hits a triangle at P. The weights of the three vertices interpolate UV coordinates and vertex normals. Arrows show vertex normals and the interpolated shading normal.</desc><defs><marker id="normal-arrow" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="5" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L6,3 L0,6" fill="none" stroke="#657a42" /></marker><pattern id="mesh-grid" width="22" height="22" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><path d="M22 0H0V22" fill="none" stroke="#d3d8c5" stroke-width="1" /></pattern></defs><path d="M65 245 L350 245 L230 85 Z" fill="url(#mesh-grid)" stroke="#657a42" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M225 190L65 245 M225 190L350 245 M225 190L230 85" fill="none" stroke="#99a684" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><g stroke="#657a42" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#normal-arrow)"><path d="M65 245L43 181"/><path d="M350 245L375 183"/><path d="M230 85L245 27"/><path d="M225 190L230 122"/></g><path d="M95 85L225 190" stroke="#89917b" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/><g fill="#657a42"><circle cx="65" cy="245" r="4"/><circle cx="350" cy="245" r="4"/><circle cx="230" cy="85" r="4"/><circle cx="225" cy="190" r="5"/></g><g fill="#48583a" font-family="monospace" font-size="12"><text x="42" y="274">v₀ / UV₀</text><text x="304" y="274">v₁ / UV₁</text><text x="153" y="78">v₂ / UV₂</text><text x="238" y="196">P</text><text x="245" y="130">n shading</text><text x="60" y="73">incoming ray</text></g></svg>
        <div class="interpolation"><span>w₀ = 1 − α − β · w₁ = α · w₂ = β</span><p>UV(P) = Σ wᵢ UVᵢ<br />n(P) = normalize(Σ wᵢ nᵢ)</p></div><p class="fine">Conceptual diagram of the implemented attribute interpolation; not a wireframe or normal pass of Spot.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="mesh-capabilities">
        <article><span>01 / INGEST</span><h3>OBJ & MTL loading</h3><p>Read mesh faces and their vertex attributes; connect diffuse material textures to the resulting triangle primitives.</p></article>
        <article><span>02 / INTERSECT</span><h3>Triangle intersection</h3><p>Intersect the triangle plane and test barycentric coordinates. Reject hits outside the face; retain the weights for shading attributes.</p></article>
        <article><span>03 / INTERPOLATE</span><h3>UVs & vertex normals</h3><p>Interpolate UVs and optional vertex normals at the hit. Normalize the shading normal and align it with the geometric normal before orienting it to the ray.</p></article>
        <article><span>04 / ACCELERATE</span><h3>Per-triangle bounds & BVH</h3><p>Give each triangle an AABB, then build a mesh hierarchy. The same imported geometry becomes the workload for the median-versus-SAH study.</p><a href="#acceleration">Next: acceleration results ↓</a></article>
      </div>
    </div></section>
    <section id="acceleration" class="chapter wrap">
      <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / ACCELERATION STRUCTURES</p><h2>More triangles.<br />Fewer intersection tests.</h2><p>With imported meshes working, dense models such as the 871,306-triangle Stanford Dragon made intersection cost a practical constraint. I implemented a binned surface area heuristic to choose partitions by estimated tracing cost.</p></header>
      <div class="geometry-showcase"><figure><img src="/projects/path-tracer/dragon.png" alt="Green Stanford Dragon on a stone pedestal rendered by the path tracer" width="480" height="384" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FIG. 03 / STANFORD DRAGON · 871,306 TRIANGLES</figcaption></figure><div class="large-result"><span>DRAGON / MEDIAN BVH → SAH</span><strong>{bvh[1].render_reduction_percent.toFixed(1)}<small>%</small></strong><p>less render time</p><div>{bvh[1].median.render_s.toFixed(2)} s <span>→</span> {bvh[1].sah.render_s.toFixed(2)} s</div><p class="fine">64 SPP · one thread · median of five runs</p></div></div>
      <div class="three-notes"><article><h3>The baseline</h3><p>The existing median builder splits primitives by count along the longest bounding-box axis. Balanced counts do not necessarily produce low-cost traversal.</p></article><article><h3>The extension</h3><p>Evaluate 12 centroid bins per axis. Child surface areas and primitive counts estimate split cost, which is compared against keeping a leaf.</p></article><article><h3>The tradeoff</h3><p>Evaluating candidate partitions adds work during construction. I measured mesh build time separately from rendering to compare both sides of that decision.</p></article></div>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Two scenes · median of five runs per builder</caption><thead><tr><th>Scene / triangles</th><th>Median render</th><th>SAH render</th><th>Reduction</th><th>Mesh build: median / SAH</th></tr></thead><tbody>{bvh.map(r => <tr><th>{r.scene === 'spot' ? 'Spot' : 'Dragon'}<span>{r.triangles.toLocaleString('en-US')} triangles</span></th><td>{r.median.render_s.toFixed(3)} s</td><td>{r.sah.render_s.toFixed(3)} s</td><td class="accent">−{r.render_reduction_percent.toFixed(1)}%</td><td>{r.median.mesh_build_ms.toFixed(2)} / {r.sah.mesh_build_ms.toFixed(2)} ms</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <details><summary>Benchmark details</summary><p>Tested on Apple M2 with a C++ Release build at 64 SPP using one thread. Render times are the median of five runs and exclude mesh loading and BVH construction.</p><p>The median and SAH builders use different leaf configurations, so these results compare the complete implementations rather than isolating the split heuristic. The SAH builder was informed by <a href="https://pbr-book.org/4ed/Primitives_and_Intersection_Acceleration/Bounding_Volume_Hierarchies">PBRT</a>.</p><div class="downloads"><a href="/projects/path-tracer/bvh/spot-timings.csv">Spot benchmark data</a><a href="/projects/path-tracer/bvh/dragon-timings.csv">Dragon benchmark data</a><a href="/projects/path-tracer/bvh/README.md">How to reproduce</a></div></details>
    </section>
    <section id="reflection" class="dark-section"><div class="wrap chapter">
      <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">03 / MICROFACET REFLECTION</p><h2>A material model is only<br />half the problem.</h2><p>I extended the renderer with isotropic GGX reflection, roughness and metallic controls. Efficiently sampling that reflection lobe matters just as much as evaluating it.</p></header>
      <div class="brdf"><div><span>D</span><h3>Distribution</h3><p>GGX describes microfacet orientations; roughness changes the width of the reflection lobe.</p></div><div><span>G</span><h3>Geometry</h3><p>Masking and shadowing account for microfacets hidden along the view and light directions.</p></div><div><span>F</span><h3>Fresnel</h3><p>Angle-dependent reflectance combines with the metallic control to shape the surface response.</p></div></div>
      <div class="sampling-heading"><h3>Same BRDF. Different sampling.</h3><p>Uniform hemisphere → GGX normal distribution → GGX visible normals</p></div>
      <div class="sampler-grid">{[['uniform','Uniform hemisphere'],['ndf','GGX NDF'],['vndf','GGX VNDF']].map(([id,label]) => <figure><img src={`/projects/path-tracer/sampling/${id}.png`} alt={`${label} render at 64 samples per pixel`} width="144" height="96" loading="lazy" /><figcaption><span>{label}</span><span>64 SPP</span></figcaption></figure>)}</div>
      <div class="sampling-conclusion"><div><p class="eyebrow">OBSERVATION / ONE SCENE, FIVE SEEDS</p><h3>Why sampling strategy matters.</h3><p>At 64 SPP, VNDF’s mean linear RGB MSE was 8.4% lower than NDF’s across five seeds. This descriptive average does not establish that VNDF consistently outperforms NDF across seeds, scenes, or view configurations. The larger difference in this experiment is between uniform sampling and the two GGX proposals.</p></div></div>
      <div class="implementation-note"><p class="eyebrow">IMPLEMENTATION / KEEPING THE ESTIMATOR CONSISTENT</p><h3>From visible-normal sampling to weighted paths.</h3><p>I implemented GGX visible-normal sampling based on Eric Heitz’s <a href="https://jcgt.org/published/0007/04/01/">Sampling the GGX Distribution of Visible Normals (2018)</a>.</p><p>Integrating the method into my path tracer, I converted the visible microfacet-normal density into a reflection-direction PDF using the half-vector Jacobian, 1 / (4 |ωo · h|). That PDF participates in the material’s specular/diffuse proposal and the integrator’s light/material mixture; path contributions use the full mixture density.</p><p>Below-surface reflected directions remain zero-contribution samples rather than being resampled. Keeping generation, PDF evaluation, and rejection behavior consistent was essential; numerical checks compare the sampler and PDF, and reflected energy against quadrature.</p></div>
      <details><summary>Sampling results & implementation notes</summary><p>The three sampling strategies were compared using the same GGX BRDF and scene settings at 64 SPP. Each result summarizes five random seeds, with mean squared error measured against a high-sample reference image in unclamped linear RGB.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>64 SPP · five seeds · mean ± sample standard deviation</caption><thead><tr><th>Sampler</th><th>Linear RGB MSE</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody>{samples.map(r => <tr><th>{labels[r.sampler as keyof typeof labels]}</th><td>{r.mse_mean.toExponential(3)} ± {r.mse_sd.toExponential(3)}</td><td>{r.seconds_mean.toFixed(3)} ± {r.seconds_sd.toFixed(3)} s</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><figure><img src="/projects/path-tracer/sampling/convergence.svg" alt="Convergence chart showing mean error by sample count for three samplers" width="850" height="480" loading="lazy" /></figure><p>VNDF showed slightly lower average error than NDF in this scene, although the difference was modest. Numerical tests also checked sampling/PDF consistency and reflected energy against reference calculations. The reference image averages two independent 32,768-SPP renders.</p><div class="downloads"><a href="/projects/path-tracer/sampling/runs.csv">Raw sampling results</a><a href="/projects/path-tracer/sampling/results.json">Detailed results</a><a href="/projects/path-tracer/sampling/reproduce.md">Experiment setup & validation</a></div></details>
    </div></section>
    <section id="parallelism" class="chapter wrap">
      <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">04 / MULTITHREADING & PERFORMANCE</p><h2>More threads.<br />Not always more speed.</h2><p>I added workers that claim rows from an atomic counter, then compared thread counts against an identical workload.</p></header>
      <div class="parallel-layout"><div><div class="speedup"><strong>{best.speedup.toFixed(2)}×</strong><span>speedup with four threads</span></div><div class="bars">{rows.map(r => <div class:list={['bar-row',{best:r.threads===4}]}><span>{r.threads}T</span><div class="track"><div style={`width:${r.median_s / baseline.median_s * 100}%`}></div></div><b>{r.median_s.toFixed(2)} s</b></div>)}</div><p class="fine">Median of five runs · lower is better · deterministic pixel streams</p></div><figure><img src="/projects/path-tracer/cornell-clean.png" alt="Cornell Box render with a glass sphere and tall box" width="240" height="240" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FIG. 04 / CORNELL BOX · 240 × 240 · 64 SPP<br />Illustrative render; benchmark scene differs slightly</figcaption></figure></div>
      <div class="three-notes"><article><h3>Distribute the work</h3><p>Workers claim rows, write disjoint pixels to a shared buffer, and join before ordered image output.</p></article><article><h3>Keep the image fixed</h3><p>Optional deterministic pixel-seeded random streams make output independent of worker count and scheduling. All compared images are byte-identical.</p></article><article><h3>Report the limit</h3><p>Four threads achieved the best measured performance; eight workers were slightly slower. The measurements show a scaling limit, but do not isolate its cause.</p></article></div>
      <p class="hardware-note">Benchmarks were conducted on an Apple M2 with <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/111867">four performance cores and four efficiency cores</a>. Four workers achieved the best measured performance, while eight were slightly slower. The heterogeneous core configuration may contribute to this behavior, but these measurements do not isolate core placement, scheduling overhead, resource contention, or other system-level effects.</p>
      <details><summary>Performance notes</summary><p>Benchmarks were run on an Apple M2 using a C++ Release build. Each configuration was measured five times after a warmup run; the chart reports median render times.</p><p>A pixel-seeded random number generator keeps the rendered image identical across thread counts, so differences reflect execution time rather than changes in sampling.</p><div class="downloads"><a href="/projects/path-tracer/threads/runs.csv">Raw benchmark data</a><a href="/projects/path-tracer/threads/results.json">Detailed results</a><a href="/projects/path-tracer/threads/README.md">How to reproduce</a></div></details>
    </section>
    <section id="architecture" class="chapter wrap"><header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">05 / RENDERER ARCHITECTURE</p><h2>One renderer.<br />Connected extensions.</h2><p>Each extension changes a part of the path from camera sample to accumulated radiance.</p></header><ol class="architecture">{[['Camera rays','Parallel workers claim rows from an atomic counter.','MULTITHREADING'],['Traversal & intersection','SAH partitions imported triangles into a mesh hierarchy.','MESHES + SAH'],['BSDF & light sampling','Evaluate reflection and sample the next path direction.','GGX + NDF / VNDF'],['Integration & film','Accumulate weighted radiance and write the final image.','PATH TRACING']].map(([title,text,tag],i) => <li><span class="stage">0{i+1} →</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p><span class="stage-tag">{tag}</span></li>)}</ol><div class="closing"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT THIS PROJECT TAUGHT ME</p><p>Implementing a feature is the beginning. Understanding it means checking the image, isolating the workload, measuring the tradeoff, and being precise about what the result proves.</p></div></section>
  </main>
  <footer class="wrap"><div><a class="wordmark" href="/">CHAO BIAN</a><a href="/#projects">Back to all projects ↑</a></div><p class="eyebrow">REFERENCES & ASSET CREDITS</p><p>Eric Heitz. <a href="https://jcgt.org/published/0007/04/01/">Sampling the GGX Distribution of Visible Normals</a>. Journal of Computer Graphics Techniques, 7(4), 2018.</p><p>Foundation: <a href="https://raytracing.github.io/">Ray Tracing in One Weekend</a>, Peter Shirley and contributors. Technical reference: <a href="https://pbr-book.org/4ed/">Physically Based Rendering</a>.<br />Models: <a href="https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~kmcrane/Projects/ModelRepository/">Spot — Keenan Crane (CC0)</a>; <a href="https://graphics.stanford.edu/data/3Dscanrep/">Dragon — Stanford Computer Graphics Laboratory</a>. All renders produced with this project.</p></footer>
</body>
</html>
